  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 132 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nSaturday through Wednesday:\n\nA weak surface trough stemming from a broad upper low and\nassociated surface low crossing Ontario will sag through WI on\nSaturday. This will mainly bring a wind shift from southwest to\nwest, but the 850-700mb warm air advection and weak surface\nconvergence (enhanced by the lake breeze) may help trigger a \nfew showers or thunderstorms in the afternoon and evening. \nSaturday high temps will be in the mid 80s with dewpoints \ncreeping back into the 60s. It will feel warm and humid. \n\nSunday afternoon and night, and potentially into Monday, look to\nbring some more unsettled weather into the area. Overall, there\nwill be another broad, warm and humid ridge over the center of\nthe country. This ridge should push into at least the southwest\nhalf of WI on Sunday and bump our highs to around 90 with \ndewpoints pushing 70 (very warm and humid). Meanwhile, a mid \nlevel shortwave trough is expected to slide across the \nUS/Canadian border from MN to Lake Superior to Lake Ontario,\ncoincident with an 850mb baroclinic zone. It looks like\ningredients (moisture, instability, shear) will be in place over\nat least central WI Sunday afternoon/night for the potential for\na round of severe storms. The forcing would be the nose of the\nlow level jet and shortwave trough. There is more uncertainty\nfor how far south the storms will get Sunday night.\n\nThe ridge will hang on over the central U.S. through much of the\nnext week. Several more shortwave troughs are expected to track\nacross the Upper Great Lakes from Monday through Wednesday, so\nadditional rounds of storms (possibly severe) are likely for\ncentral and southern WI. Each round will depend on how the\nprevious day's storms panned out. This looks to be an active\nweather pattern setting up for WI. \n\nCronce",
